        {
          "id": "SP_HEMIASTERELLA_COMPLICATA_TOPSENT_1919",
          "name": "Hemiasterella complicata Topsent, 1919",
          "rank": "SPECIES",
          "commonName": "",
          "lineage": "Hemiasterella",
          "subspeciesCount": 0,
          "description": "The sponge is lamellate, foliose; color is dark red to violet (Fig. 27 A – B). On the surface, two specimens present algal turf. The samples preserved in alcohol changed color to beige; the dried samples are whitish, light brown; in the dried state, spicule tracts create an irregular pattern and thick lines on the surface. The samples have a micro-bristly surface and an elastic consistence. SKELETON. The ectosome is constituted of a layer of densely packed euasters (Fig. 27 C). the choanosome with plumo-reticulate tracts is formed by megascleres (Fig. 27 D), without a well-marked differentiation between axial and extra-axial regions. SPICULES. The megascleres are constituted by strongyloxeas and oxeas (Fig. 27 E), with a wide range of intermediate forms, 590 – (842.5, 100.8) – 980 µm × 10 – (17.25, 4.3) – 25.5 µm; strongylasters with at least five rays (Fig. 27 F) with microspined rays 7.5 – (12.4, 3.6) – 20 µm, and smooth oxyasters 5 – (8.9, 3.8) – 25 µm (Fig. 27 G).",
          "sourcedFrom": "gbif",
          "wikipediaTitle": "gbif:2250833",
          "children": [],
          "uuid": "35dd20e3-8fec-559-80e-f554e145257e6f",
          "sourceIds": {
